window.docContents[53].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-linear-maps/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___11___-Tensor-products--Tensor-product-of-linear-maps":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products","header":"Tensor product of linear maps","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Tensor-product-of-linear-maps/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___11___-Tensor-products--Tensor-product-of-linear-maps"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras--Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations--Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Declaration: IsOfFinOrder\n\nAlignment metadata: book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting\n\nThe complex algebra determined by the displayed parameter-dependent relations.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nThe parameter-dependent relations on pairs of elements of the free algebra.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]The two weight generators multiply to one in this order.The two weight generators multiply to one in the reverse order.Moving the weight generator past the raising generator scales by q ^ 2.Moving the weight generator past the lowering generator scales by (q ^ 2)⁻¹.The raising-lowering commutator is controlled by the difference of weight generators.\n\nThe distinguished complex-valued algebra homomorphism is surjective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nA finite-dimensional simple module at a finite-order parameter has finrank at most the parameter order when its square is not one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nThe inverse weight element multiplied by the weight element is one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nOn a finite-dimensional simple module, the order-th power of the lowering element acts by a scalar when the squared parameter is not one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nScaling the raising-lowering commutator by the difference of the parameter and its inverse gives the difference of the weight elements.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nOn a finite-dimensional simple module, the order-th power of the raising element acts by a scalar when the squared parameter is not one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nOn a finite-dimensional simple module at a finite-order parameter, the weight-action endomorphism is semisimple, its eigenspaces span, and a common positive power of its eigenvalues is fixed.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nThe weight element multiplied by its inverse element is one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nMoving the weight element past the lowering element introduces the inverse square of the parameter.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nMoving the weight element past the raising element introduces the square of the parameter.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\nOn a finite-dimensional simple module at a finite-order parameter, the order-th power of the weight element acts by a nonzero scalar.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter2/Problem2.16.5; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.16. Problems on Lie algebras\u0009Quantum enveloping algebra U_q(sl(2)) and its irreducible representations\u0009Quantum enveloping algebra U_q(sl(2)) and its irreducible representations\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras--Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations--Quantum-enveloping-algebra-U_q_LPAR_sl_LPAR_2_RPAR__RPAR_-and-its-irreducible-representations--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations","header":"Examples of representations: zero, regular, k, free algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory--2___3___-Representations--Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___13___-Historical-interlude___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry/Section-4___13___-Historical-interlude-___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___13___-Historical-interlude___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry--Section-4___13___-Historical-interlude-___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.13. Historical interlude: William Rowan Hamilton's quaternion of geometry, algebra, metaphysics, and poetry","header":"Section 4.13: Historical interlude — William Rowan Hamilton's quaternion of geometry, algebra, metaphysics, and poetry","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___13___-Historical-interlude___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry/Section-4___13___-Historical-interlude-___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___13___-Historical-interlude___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry--Section-4___13___-Historical-interlude-___-William-Rowan-Hamilton___s-quaternion-of-geometry___-algebra___-metaphysics___-and-poetry"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-the-quaternion-group-Q_8/#Chapter4___Example4___3_Q8":{"contents":"(3) The quaternion group Q_8 = \\{\\pm 1, \\pm i, \\pm j, \\pm k\\}, with defining relations\n\ni = jk = -kj, \\quad j = ki = -ik, \\quad k = ij = -ji, \\quad -1 = i^2 = j^2 = k^2.\n\nThe five conjugacy classes are \\{1\\}, \\{-1\\}, \\{\\pm i\\}, \\{\\pm j\\}, \\{\\pm k\\}, so there are five different irreducible representations, the sum of the squares of whose dimensions is 8, so their dimensions must be 1, 1, 1, 1, and 2.\nThe center Z(Q_8) is \\{\\pm 1\\}, and Q_8/Z(Q_8) \\cong \\mathbb{Z}_2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_2. The four 1-dimensional irreducible representations of \\mathbb{Z}_2 \\times \\mathbb{Z}_2 can be \"pulled back\" to Q_8. That is, if q : Q_8 \\to Q_8/Z(Q_8) is the quotient map and \\rho is any representation of Q_8/Z(Q_8), then \\rho \\circ q gives a representation of Q_8.\n\nThe 2-dimensional representation is V = \\mathbb{C}^2, given by \\rho(-1) = -\\mathrm{Id} and\n\n(4.3.1) \\qquad \\rho(i) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\rho(j) = \\begin{pmatrix} \\sqrt{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\sqrt{-1} \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad \\rho(k) = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -\\sqrt{-1} \\\\ -\\sqrt{-1} & 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\nThese are the Pauli matrices, which arise in quantum mechanics.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Irreducible representations of the quaternion group Q_8","header":"Irreducible representations of the quaternion group Q_8","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Irreducible-representations-of-the-quaternion-group-Q_8/#Chapter4___Example4___3_Q8"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Proof-of-Theorem-5___12___2-using-Lemmas-5___13___1-5___13___4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--Proof-of-Theorem-5___12___2-using-Lemmas-5___13___1-5___13___4":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$","header":"Proof of Theorem 5.12.2 using Lemmas 5.13.1-5.13.4","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Proof-of-Theorem-5___12___2-using-Lemmas-5___13___1-5___13___4/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___--Proof-of-Theorem-5___12___2-using-Lemmas-5___13___1-5___13___4"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Derivation-of-dimension-formula-for-V_lambda-and-definition-of-hook/#Chapter5___Discussion_hook_length_derivation___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.17. The hook length formula\u0009Derivation of dimension formula for V_lambda and definition of hook\u0009Derivation of dimension formula for V_lambda and definition of hook","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___17___-The-hook-length-formula/Derivation-of-dimension-formula-for-V_lambda-and-definition-of-hook/#Chapter5___Discussion_hook_length_derivation___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Endomorphism-algebras-and-real-type-characterization/#Chapter5___Problem5___1___2":{"contents":"Problem 5.1.2. (a) Show that \\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb{R}[G]} V is \\mathbb{C} for V of complex type, \\operatorname{Mat}_2(\\mathbb{R}) for V of real type, and \\mathbb{H} for V of quaternionic type, which motivates the names above.\n\nHint: Show that the complexification V_{\\mathbb{C}} of V decomposes as V \\oplus V^*. Use this to compute the dimension of \\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb{R}[G]} V in all three cases. Using the fact that \\mathbb{C} \\subset \\operatorname{End}_{\\mathbb{R}[G]} V, prove the result in the\ncomplex case. In the remaining two cases, let B be the invariant bilinear form on V and let ( \\ , \\ ) be the invariant positive Hermitian form (they are defined up to a nonzero complex scalar and a positive real scalar, respectively). Define the operator j : V \\to V such that B(v, w) = (v, jw). Show that j is complex antilinear (ji = -ij), and j^2 = \\lambda \\cdot \\operatorname{Id}, where \\lambda is a real number, positive in the real case and negative in the quaternionic case (if B is renormalized, j multiplies by a nonzero complex number z and j^2 by z\\bar{z}, as j is antilinear). Thus j can be normalized so that j^2 = 1 in the real case and j^2 = -1 in the quaternionic case. Deduce the claim from this.\n\n(b) Show that V is of real type if and only if V is the complexification of a representation V_{\\mathbb{R}} over the field of real numbers.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.1. Frobenius-Schur indicator\u0009Endomorphism algebras and real type characterization","header":"Endomorphism algebras and real type characterization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___1___-Frobenius-Schur-indicator/Endomorphism-algebras-and-real-type-characterization/#Chapter5___Problem5___1___2"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials/#Chapter5___Proposition5___21___1":{"contents":"Proposition 5.21.1.\n\n\\prod_m (x_1^m + \\cdots + x_N^m)^{i_m} = \\sum_{\\lambda: p \\leq N} \\chi_\\lambda(C_\\mathbf{i}) S_\\lambda(x).\n\nProof. We will prove that\n\n\\Delta(x) \\prod_{m \\geq 1} (x_1^m + \\cdots + x_N^m)^{i_m} = \\sum_{\\lambda: p \\leq N} \\chi_\\lambda(C_\\mathbf{i}) D_\\lambda(x)\n\n(where the sum is over partitions of n with at most N parts) by comparing the coefficients of x^\\nu for all \\nu = (\\nu_1, \\ldots, \\nu_N). Both sides are antisymmetric polynomials, so it is enough to consider the case in which \\nu_1 > \\cdots > \\nu_N. Also, both sides are homogeneous polynomials of degree (N - 1) + (N - 2) + \\cdots + 1 + n, so we may assume that \\nu_1 + \\cdots + \\nu_N equals this. Let \\rho = (N - 1, \\ldots, 1, 0); then \\nu = \\mu + \\rho where \\mu_1 \\geq \\cdots \\geq \\mu_N \\geq 0 and \\mu_1 + \\cdots + \\mu_N = n.\n\nThe coefficient of x^\\nu on the left side is \\chi_\\mu(C_\\mathbf{i}) by the Frobenius character formula. The only monomial in D_\\lambda(x) with strictly decreasing exponents is x^{\\lambda + \\rho} (the \\sigma = 1 term in the determinant), so the coefficient of x^\\nu in the right side is the coefficient of x^{\\mu + \\rho} in \\sum_{\\lambda: p \\leq N} \\chi_\\lambda(C_\\mathbf{i}) x^{\\lambda + \\rho}, which again is \\chi_\\mu(C_\\mathbf{i}): only the \\lambda = \\mu term contributes. \\square\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.21. Schur polynomials\u0009Character expansion in terms of Schur polynomials","header":"Character expansion in terms of Schur polynomials","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials/#Chapter5___Proposition5___21___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Q-span-equivalent-to-C-span-in-Artin___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Remark5___26___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.26. Artin's theorem\u0009Q-span equivalent to C-span in Artin's theorem\u0009Q-span equivalent to C-span in Artin's theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___26___-Artin___s-theorem/Q-span-equivalent-to-C-span-in-Artin___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Remark5___26___2___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Ind_H___G-V-is-isomorphic-to-Hom_H_LPAR_k_LSQ_G_RSQ____-V_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___8___-Induced-representations--Ind_H___G-V-is-isomorphic-to-Hom_H_LPAR_k_LSQ_G_RSQ____-V_RPAR_--Ind_H___G-V-is-isomorphic-to-Hom_H_LPAR_k_LSQ_G_RSQ____-V_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The isomorphism from induction to coinduction for a finite-index subgroup.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.8.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe natural isomorphism between induction and coinduction functors for a finite-index subgroup.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.8.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe component of the induction--coinduction functor isomorphism is the representation isomorphism.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.8.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.8. Induced representations\u0009Ind_H^G V is isomorphic to Hom_H(k[G], V)\u0009Ind_H^G V is isomorphic to Hom_H(k[G], V)\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Ind_H___G-V-is-isomorphic-to-Hom_H_LPAR_k_LSQ_G_RSQ____-V_RPAR_/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___8___-Induced-representations--Ind_H___G-V-is-isomorphic-to-Hom_H_LPAR_k_LSQ_G_RSQ____-V_RPAR_--Ind_H___G-V-is-isomorphic-to-Hom_H_LPAR_k_LSQ_G_RSQ____-V_RPAR_--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Flat-modules/#Chapter8___Problem8___1___3___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Flat modules\u0009Flat modules","header":"Formalization","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Flat-modules/#Chapter8___Problem8___1___3___formalization"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Categories-without-enough-projectives/#Chapter8___Exercise8___2___9":{"contents":"Exercise 8.2.9. (i) Show that the category of finite abelian groups or finite dimensional k[x]-modules does not contain nonzero projective objects (so it does not have enough projectives).\n\n(ii) Let A be a finitely generated commutative ring. Show that the category of finitely generated A-modules has enough projectives.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.2. Tor and Ext functors\u0009Categories without enough projectives","header":"Categories without enough projectives","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___2___-Tor-and-Ext-functors/Categories-without-enough-projectives/#Chapter8___Exercise8___2___9"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Cartan-matrix/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra--Cartan-matrix--Cartan-matrix--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary natural-number matrix associated with a family of modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Definition9.3.1; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Cartan matrix\u0009Cartan matrix\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Cartan-matrix/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra--Cartan-matrix--Cartan-matrix--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra--Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra--Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"A finite projective module satisfying the displayed condition is equivalent to one of the two projective modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.3.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe negative projective module is projective and its displayed quotient has the stated cover properties.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.3.2; role=supporting]\n\nNo algebra module is simultaneously equivalent to both signed simple modules.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.3.2; role=supporting]\n\nThe positive projective module is projective and its displayed quotient has the stated cover properties.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.3.2; role=supporting]\n\nA finite projective module satisfying the displayed condition is equivalent to a unique member of the projective family.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.3.2; role=supporting]\n\nEvery compatible simple algebra module is equivalent to the positive or negative simple module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Problem9.3.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.3. The Cartan matrix of a finite dimensional algebra\u0009Simple modules, projective modules, and Cartan matrix of a specific algebra\u0009Simple modules, projective modules, and Cartan matrix of a specific algebra\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra/Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___3___-The-Cartan-matrix-of-a-finite-dimensional-algebra--Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra--Simple-modules___-projective-modules___-and-Cartan-matrix-of-a-specific-algebra--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories--Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.6. Finite abelian categories","header":"Finite abelian categories are module categories","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories/Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___6___-Finite-abelian-categories--Finite-abelian-categories-are-module-categories"}});